<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Delian League</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/478-bce">478</a>–<a href="/years/404-bce">404 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Delian League was an alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> founded in 478 BCE under Athenian leadership to continue the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, with its treasury first on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a>. Members contributed ships or money, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> gradually turned the league into an empire, moving the treasury to Athens in 454 BCE. It was dissolved after Athens' defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> in 404 BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the Greek victories at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> and Mycale in 479 BCE, the allied fleet besieged Sestos and then captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantion">Byzantion</a> in 478 BCE, but the behaviour of the Spartan general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a> alienated many allies and he was recalled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> was then eager to end its part in the war. Its king Leotychidas had proposed moving all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, but the Athenian commander Xanthippus rejected this, saying the Athenians would protect the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a>, who had been their colonists.</p><p>A congress on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a> then founded a new alliance. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> its official aim was to 'avenge the wrongs they suffered by ravaging the territory of the king'. Members swore to have the same friends and enemies and dropped iron ingots into the sea to symbolise the permanence of the alliance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Members could provide ships or pay a tax into the common treasury, and most chose to pay. The Athenian politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristides">Aristides</a> was occupied with fixing the tax of new members. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a> the League expelled the remaining Persian forces from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, and Cimon forced Karystos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> to join and conquered Skyros. Athens treated attempts to leave harshly. Naxos was defeated in about 471 BCE, and Thasos, which seceded in 465 BCE after Athens founded Amphipolis near its mines, surrendered after more than two years of siege, lost its walls, ships and mines and had to pay tribute.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> saw this as the point where the alliance became a hegemony. In 454 BCE Pericles moved the treasury to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, allegedly to keep it safe from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> records that his rivals saw it as using the money for building projects such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a>.</p><p>In 460 BCE Athens sent a large fleet to support a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and the expedition was destroyed; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isocrates">Isocrates</a> puts the loss at some 20,000 men and modern estimates at 50,000 men and 250 ships. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a> died during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of Citium on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. Some writers report a peace with Persia, the Peace of Callias, in 450 BCE, while others think it a later invention.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> began to plant kleruchiai, quasi-colonies serving as garrisons, and officials supervised the tribute. After an unsuccessful revolt, Mytilene on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> lost the land of the whole island to Athenian settlers, and Melos was conquered and its men put to death. By 431 BCE the League's threat to Spartan hegemony and Athens's heavy administration led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Spartans, aided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and by Athenian internal strife, defeated the Athenian empire after 27 years of war, and the League was dissolved in 404 BCE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a>. Athens founded the Second Athenian League in 377 BCE against Spartan and then Theban power but never recovered its former strength.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Delian League” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Naval power, rowed largely by the poorest citizens, the thetes, became the basis of Athenian strength and of the <b>Delian League</b>: under Cimon the allies destroyed a Persian fleet and army at the Battle of the Eurymedon around 466 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>As a member of the <b>Delian League</b> Byzantium paid one of the highest tribute assessments in the Hellespontine district, reflecting its wealth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Battle of Mycale in 479 BCE Chios joined the Greek alliance and then the <b>Delian League</b>, providing ships rather than tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Delian League</b> was the alliance of Aegean Greek city-states that Athens led against Persia from 478 BC.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>As a member of the <b>Delian League</b>, Ephesus paid tribute to Athens rather than supplying warships.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After its refoundation Miletus belonged to the <b>Delian League</b>, and an Athenian decree of about 450 BCE regulated its government, probably after a period of oligarchic unrest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
